\section{Related Work}
\label{section:related}

\citet{kryscinski-etal-2019-neural} presented a model which is most similar to our own: they proposed a model which consists of a simple sequence-to-sequence neural network augmented by a copy mechanism to generate sentences from the abstractive summarisation task. However, the authors found that the copy mechanism did not work as expected, and it was unclear how to incorporate explicit constraints into the model.

# Simplification of short texts

## Overview

The task consists in transforming a sentence into a shorter version of the same text, i.e., a summary of the sentence in order to simplify the sentence while preserving the same content. This task has a wide range of application areas:

• the simplification of the writing style of children's books so that they can be read by children;
• the simplification of long legal texts, such as judicial opinions, to facilitate the understanding of the content of the text and make the laws more accessible to the public;

\subsection{Evaluation of the Results}
We use the official scoring toolkit \cite{wmt21metrics} of WMT 21 summarization competition for evaluating the results.
For English to French, we use two types of automatic evaluation metrics: ROUGE-L (F1 score) and METEOR. For French to English, we use BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy).
